Program Report: African Open Source Wikipedia Initiative
Project Name: African Open Source Wikipedia Initiative 
Program Manager: Rahmat Akintola 
Duration: April 5th – April 26th, 2025 (4 Weeks) 
Organizers: Everything Open Source & Wikipedia User Group Uganda 
Partner: CHAOSS Africa
Executive Summary
The African Open Source Wikipedia Initiative was an intensive four-week virtual program conducted via Discord aimed at documenting and amplifying the contributions of African open-source pioneers. By engaging 45 participants across 7 different countries, the initiative successfully bridged the representation gap for African tech innovators on global knowledge platforms like Wikipedia and Wikidata.
Engagement & Participation
* Total Registered: 45 participants from 7 countries.
* Community Profile: The cohort included participants from diverse backgrounds, including developers, technical writers, and digital literacy advocates from nations such as Ghana, Nigeria, Algeria, and the United States.
* Capacity Building: 24 new Wikipedia editors were onboarded, with 14 maintaining active editor status throughout the program.
* Training: Applicants were vetted based on prior open-source experience and writing skills, with many completing introductory courses on Open Source and Wikipedia editing.
Impact Metrics
The program exceeded its documentation goals, significantly increasing the digital footprint of African open source projects:
* Content Creation: 106 total articles were created across the initiative.
* Editing Activity: 406 articles were edited, contributing to a total of 2.78k edits throughout the campaign.
* Substantial Growth: Contributors added over 155k words and uploaded 97 common files to enhance article quality.
* Reach: Articles documented during this period have already garnered over 48.7k views.
Key Deliverables & Approved Data
The initiative focused on creating high-quality, verified data entries for key figures and organizations:
* Wikipedia Articles: 3 comprehensive Wikipedia articles were fully approved.
* Wikidata for People: 22 new Wikidata entries created for prominent open-source individuals, including leaders like Marlene Mhangami, Samson Goddy, and Angela Oduor Lungati.
* Wikidata for Organizations/Projects: 13 entries created for essential projects such as Women in Technology Uganda, CHAOSS, and the Python Software Association in Namibia.
Conclusion
The African Open Source Wikipedia Initiative has set a strong foundation for the continuous documentation of Africa’s role in global innovation. By training a new generation of editors and formalizing the records of African contributors, we have ensured that their impact is recognized, searchable, and preserved for the global tech community.


