Celebrating the African Open Source Wikipedia Initiative!
The African Open Source Wikipedia Initiative was a four-week virtual intensive program dedicated to documenting the pioneers of the African Open source ecosystem. Positioned at the intersection of open source and open knowledge, the initiative addressed the historical underrepresentation of African developers, community leads, and innovators on global platforms.
Throughout April 2025, we set out to change that narrative, and the results speak for themselves.
Program Impact at a Glance
Our 1st cohort of 45 contributors from seven countries—including Nigeria, Ghana, and Algeria collaborated via Discord,  supported by Everything Open Source, Wikipedia User Group Uganda, and CHAOSS Africa, our participants achieved the following:
* Global Reach: Articles created or improved during this sprint have already reached over 48,700 views.
* Content Creation: A staggering 106 new articles were created and 406 articles were edited.
* Knowledge Depth: We added over 155,000 words to the global knowledge base and uploaded 97 media files to Wikimedia Commons.
* Bridging the Gap: We successfully created 22 new Wikidata entries for African tech leaders and 13 entries for vital organizations like Women in Technology Uganda and the Python Software Association in Namibia.
Building Sustainable Editor Capacity
Beyond data entry, the program focused on high-level skill acquisition. The initiative onboarded 24 new Wikipedia editors, with 14 maintaining active status. Participants were vetted for prior open-source experience and trained in Wikipedia’s specific editing guidelines, establishing a community of digital archivists equipped to maintain these records. 
We also had a Guest speaker session with Afi Abigail Gbadago sharing her experience on Open Source tech contributions for Wikipedia.


Why This Matters
Documentation is a form of legacy. By ensuring that leaders like Marlene Mhangami, Samson Goddy, and Angela Oduor Lungati have verified, searchable profiles on Wikipedia and Wikidata, we are making it easier for the world to discover, cite, and be inspired by African innovation.
What’s Next?
While the formal four-week program has concluded, the work of documenting our ecosystem never stops. This initiative has laid the groundwork for a more inclusive digital history, and we encourage everyone to keep editing, keep writing, and keep sharing the African open-source story. 
